#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Dotting inside Qur\'anic citations, from the September 2026 collation.

The collation read every bracketed span in both text fields of all 56 lessons
against the Warsh reference and found 6,320 places where the printing diverges
in a way that is not orthography, not a canonical variant and not paraphrase.
2,267 of those are a dot: the printed word and the aya word have the same
skeleton and differ only in where the dots sit.

This script writes 2102 of them and REVERTS 160 that an earlier version of
it wrote and should not have.

ONE SITE IN THIS TABLE WAS WRONG, AND THE ATTRIBUTION IS WHY

Lesson 19 arabicBody wrote `min` for the scan's `mi` on the collation's
attribution of that span to Q 2:133.  The span is Q 7:163, `idh ya`duna FI
l-sabt`: the paragraph reads Q 7:160-163 in order and the next bracketed span
is Q 7:163 `wa-yawma la yasbitun`.  The row now writes `fi`, and it accepts the
committed `min` beside the scan's `mi` as a starting form, so that a tree in
either state reaches the same place.  Mim for fa is not a dot, which is why the
`mi`/`fi` pass in repair-aya-settled-readings.py had left the site alone.

The row covers THREE words, not one, because the wrong aya had also HIDDEN a
repair.  Aligned against Q 2:133 the span produced four verdicts and every one
of them was an artefact of the attribution:

    printed     against Q 2:133         against Q 7:163
    ya`dul      qal, 4 apart, grade B   ya`dun -- ONE LETTER, lam for nun
    ya`tadun    ta`budun, grade A       the compiler's gloss, not Qur'anic
    mi          min, grade A            fi
    al-sabt     al-ilahak, 3 apart, B   al-sabt -- identical, no defect at all

So the pass wrote two words that were not damaged, left the one word that was,
and graded a sound word a defect.  `ya`dul` for `ya`dun` is lam for nun, inside
the eight classes the earliest scripts declare, and it sat unrepaired only
because a wrong aya had pushed it into grade B, which is out of every pass's
scope.  Both words are written together here: a repair that leaves the damaged
word standing beside the one it fixes is worse than no repair.

That failure is not unique to this site, and it was swept for.  Of the 2,655
grade-B rows the neighbour rule can evaluate 252 -- the rest are footnotes the
matcher does not read, or paragraphs with no shipped attribution on both sides.
It finds the run holding the cited aya at 184 and DENYING it at 68.  Of those
68: three become a ONE-DOT repair inside the declared classes once the span is
read against the aya the paragraph brackets, two become two-dot repairs, two are
sound words the wrong aya had graded a defect, eighteen face a word no dots can
reach, and the remaining forty-three do not align at all.

The three are not written here, because re-grading a B row means re-deriving it
from a different aya and that belongs in a pass with the guards on:

    L14 arabicBody @42826  fa-law  -> qalu    Q 5:72, cited as Q 5:17
    L21 arabicBody @36315  illa    -> inna    Q 9:7,  cited as Q 3:76
    L51 arabicBody @19977  al-fawm -> al-qawm Q 63:6, cited as Q 2:26

L14 shows why they matter.  The phrase reads `la-qad KABARA lladhIRa FALAW inna
llaha huwa l-masihu IBRu maryam` for Q 5:72 `la-qad kafara lladhina qalu inna
llaha huwa l-masihu bnu maryam`.  Four words are damaged.  One of them, `ibru`
for `ibnu`, was graded A and is repaired; the other three were graded B by the
same wrong attribution and are not.  Repairing one word of four and leaving the
rest is the shape of failure this project has had to undo twice.

WHY THERE ARE REVERTS

The first version claimed to check each site against two independent
attributions -- the collation\'s aya and the one scripts/match-verses.js gives
the same span -- and to refuse where they named different words. That check ran
on 474 sites of 2,263. On the other 1,789 the join found no second attribution
at all, and the pass reported success anyway, because nothing in it
distinguished "checked and agreed" from "nothing to check". Thirteen wrong
words reached primary text, and an audit that read all 2,262 found them.

Three guards now stand where that one did.

  A. The collation records, per site, WHICH LETTERS it found damaged. If this
     pass arrives at a different word from the one the collation names, the two
     disagree about the repair itself and the site is refused. 14 sites --
     including `ninji` at Q 19:72, where the collation says the jim is damaged,
     the first version left the ha\' alone and changed two sound letters, and
     `lanahnu` is itself a Qur\'anic word so nothing downstream would flag it.

  B. The aya has to be the best-corroborated one. Every aya holding a word with
     this word\'s folded skeleton is scored by how many of the span\'s OTHER
     words stand in it. 8 sites.

  C. Where more than one word answers the skeleton, the span must choose
     between them outright: the aya this pass reads has to hold strictly more
     of the span\'s other words than any aya offering a different word. A tie
     is a refusal. 138 sites.

     Guard C is what the three worst failures needed. `ilayna turja`un` and
     `ilayna yurja`un` both stand in the Qur\'an; the span is two words; the
     collation cited Q 29:57 and the passage is Q 40:77, which only the NEXT
     sentence settles, because it quotes Q 40:78. Nothing this pass reads can
     see that. Likewise `am yaqulun` (Q 54:44, cited as Q 2:80) and
     `hal yujzawn` (Q 27:90, cited as Q 7:147). In all three the first version
     changed a letter that was already right.

     The collation\'s summary says that for short spans "the word-level verdict
     is sound" even where the aya is only a proposal. It is not sound when the
     competing ayat carry different words.

WHAT SETTLES A KEPT SITE

The aya, corroborated by the span it sits in. Each site was re-derived from the
repository: find the printed word, take the bracketed span around it, align that
span against the aya window with the confusion classes folded, read the target
off the alignment, and require it to be reachable from the printed word by dots
alone. Alifs are ignored on both sides, because the collation rules
full-against-dagger alif orthography and the printing the authority on spelling.

THREE PAIRS ADDED

ha\'/kha\', ta\'/za\' and sin/shin, each one dot and nothing else, settled the
same way as the eight the earlier passes declare. They were missing because
nobody had read this much of the corpus. A FOURTH IS NOT ADDED: four sites are
jim for kha\', which moves a dot from below the letter to above it. On the
worklist.

TWO FURTHER REFUSALS

Lesson 8 arabicBody @12249, where the collation proposes `min` for `mi` at
Q 10:45, and complete-quranic-letter-confusions.py deliberately holds that site
as the scan left it: the compiler glosses the phrase `tudkhilu l-layl`, so the
line is Q 3:27 `tuliju l-layla FI l-nahar` and neither `min` nor `mi` is the
answer. The collation, reading the aya alone, cannot see a gloss and did not
identify the verse. Every site was checked against the rows of all seven other
scripts for that collision; there was one.

And the jim/kha\' four above.

ON THE ANCHORS

Neighbours are compared through A.dotfold, which merges eleven confusable
classes and collapses 28 letters to 14 -- lam lands in the ba\' class. The
immediate-neighbour gate is therefore weaker than it looks, and what actually
separates sites is the 70% test: every true site scores 1.00 and the closest
losing candidate anywhere reaches 0.684. That margin is thin and is the thing to
watch if these tables are ever extended.

Usage: dry run by default; pass --write to apply. Idempotent.
Sites are named by content, not by offset: see scripts/repair_anchors.py.
"""
